// src/lsp/lsp_diag.zen
// `textDocument/publishDiagnostics`: what a build found, said to a client.
//
// This is the request `design_lsp.md` §5 left when every piece under it
// had landed — "what is left is the request and a policy" — and the
// policy is the larger half. The queries are somebody else's: `Build`
// answers `diag_count`/`diag_at` for lex and parse, the `Checker` that
// `Build.whole` hands back answers the same pair for sema, and nothing
// here decides what is wrong with a program. What it decides is WHEN to
// build, WHICH file each answer belongs to, and how an answer is taken
// BACK.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHEN, AND THE DEBOUNCE THAT IS NOT ONE
//
// §5 asks for a debounce — "build after a quiet interval, not per
// `didChange`" — and a quiet interval cannot be observed by this
// server. `Stdin.read` blocks until it has exactly the bytes it asked
// for (`src/std/env/env.zen`), there is no clock capability, and
// `PLAN.md` puts threads at stage 5 while the compiler is written in
// the seed subset. A server with one thread, a blocking read and no
// clock has no way to ask either "has the user stopped typing" or "is
// there more input waiting". Pretending otherwise would be a timer that
// fires when the next keystroke arrives, which is the opposite of a
// debounce.
//
// SO THE RULE IS: A CHANGE MARKS, AND A SETTLE POINT BUILDS. `didOpen`
// and `didChange` record which document is owed a build and return; the
// transport calls `settled` when it has answered every message it
// currently holds. Three things follow, and two of them are what §5
// wanted:
//
//   - COALESCED. Every change in one batch collapses to ONE build, of
//     the LAST buffer. A superseded build is never started, which is
//     stronger than §5's "finished and its results discarded".
//   - ORDERED. There is one build at a time and it is the most recent
//     document's, so an older result cannot overwrite a newer one. The
//     race §5 names is not handled here; it is absent by construction.
//   - HONEST ABOUT THE PIPE. Over `lsp_stdio.zen` a settle point is
//     every message, because `short_by` asks for exactly one message's
//     bytes and a blocking reader cannot look past it. So over a real
//     editor this coalesces nothing yet, and the missing piece is named
//     rather than papered over: a read that can say "nothing more is
//     waiting", or a second thread. Until one exists, the cheap half is
//     below.
//
// AND THE CHEAP HALF: UNCHANGED BYTES ARE NOT A CHANGE. `lsp_serve.zen`
// marks nothing when a `didOpen` or a `didChange` carries the text the
// document already holds, which is §5's "never build on `didOpen` of a
// file already covered by the last build" narrowed to what a server can
// actually know about itself.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHICH FILE, AND WHY CLEARING IS THE HARD HALF
//
// A build reports across the whole program, so its diagnostics belong
// to many files and only one of them is open. Publishing another file's
// errors against the open document would be a wrong answer, and this
// folder's standing rule is that a wrong answer is worse than none — so
// every diagnostic is grouped by the URI its own span names and each
// URI gets its own notification.
//
// THE PROTOCOL HAS NO "REMOVE" AND THAT IS THE WHOLE PROBLEM. A
// notification REPLACES a file's list, so the only way to take an error
// back is to publish an EMPTY list for that file — and a server that
// publishes only what it found leaves the last set of errors on screen
// forever. That is the classic first-implementation bug and it is worse
// than publishing nothing at all, because the errors it leaves are ones
// the user has already fixed.
//
// So `showing` is the set of URIs currently carrying errors, and every
// build publishes for three sets: the file that was edited (always,
// empty or not), every file this build found something in, and every
// file `showing` holds that this build found nothing in. The last of
// those is the clearing, and `diagnostics_publish_and_clear` is what
// says it happens.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// SEVERITY, AND WHERE THE NOTE WENT
//
// EVERY DIAGNOSTIC IS AN ERROR, severity 1, and that is a fact about
// this compiler rather than a simplification. There is one tally —
// `Build.faults` — one exit code off it, and no phase in the tree
// produces anything a build survives. The day a warning exists, this is
// the one constant that changes.
//
// THE NOTE BECOMES `relatedInformation` AND NOT PART OF THE MESSAGE. A
// `parse.parse_diag.Note` is a sentence AND A SPAN — "a place to look
// that is not where the error is" — and folding it into the message
// throws the span away, leaving the reader told to look somewhere with
// no way to go there. `relatedInformation` is the field the protocol
// has for exactly that pair, and a client renders it as somewhere to
// jump to. It was collected and dropped until this morning; dropping it
// again one layer out would undo that.
//
// SEMA'S SECOND POSITION IS ALREADY IN ITS SENTENCE and is deliberately
// left there. A `PairFault` names two declarations and `write_detail`
// writes both, so lifting one out would be a second, divergent copy of
// how sema words a two-place diagnostic. `sema_diag.zen` owns that and
// this file only prints what it produced.

str, String = std.text
Vec, Map = std.collections
Alloc, AllocError = std.mem
Range = std.core
Span = ast.ast_span
// PARSE'S `Diag` FLAT AND SEMA'S NOT, which is `zen_build.zen`'s
// collision read one folder over: three modules export a type called
// `Diag` and `A, B = module` cannot rename. This file wants parse's
// `Note` as a value, and sema's diagnostic only long enough to render —
// so sema's fields travel as parameters and its type is never named.
Note = parse.parse_diag
Checker = sema.sema_check
SemaFault, message, write_detail = sema.sema_diag
// IMPORTED FOR ITS METHODS. `c.types` is a `Types` and `write_detail`
// takes one; without this line the bootstrapper resolves the field's
// methods against another module, exactly as `lsp_hover.zen` records.
Types = sema.sema_ty
Build = zen.zen_build
root_for, relative_to = zen.zen_path
write_range = lsp.lsp_pos
write_text, written = lsp.lsp_json
write_frame, write_decimal = lsp.lsp_frame
path_of, uri_at = lsp.lsp_uri

// ONE DIAGNOSTIC, ALREADY BELONGING TO A FILE.
//
// The `uri` is resolved at gather time and not at write time, because a
// build knows its compilation root and a writer does not — and because
// grouping by a string that is recomputed per use is grouping by a
// string that can be recomputed differently.
//
// `message` IS TEXT AND NOT A FAULT. Sema's sentence needs the `Types`
// store that decided it, and that store belongs to a `Checker` this
// value must be allowed to outlive, so it is rendered while the checker
// is still in hand. Parse's is already a `str` and is carried through.
Spot* = {
    uri*: str,
    message*: str,
    span*: Span,
    note*: Res<Note>,
}

// The server's diagnostics half: which document owes a build, and which
// files are showing errors right now.
//
// A TYPE OF ITS OWN rather than four fields on `Server`, because the
// policy above is the subject and `lsp_serve.zen` is a dispatch table.
// The server owns the documents; this owns what has been said about
// them.
Diagnostics* = {
    a: Alloc,

    // The document a build is owed to, or "" when none is. One and not
    // a queue: a build is whole-program, so the newest document's build
    // answers for every older one.
    owed :: str = "",

    // Every URI currently carrying a non-empty list. This is the ONLY
    // thing that makes clearing possible, and it holds URIs rather than
    // counts because what has to be undone is a notification.
    showing :: Vec<str>,

    // A document changed. Nothing is built here — see the header.
    owes* = (self :: @Self, uri: str) () {
        self.owed = uri;
    }

    // THE SETTLE POINT. Every message the transport held has been
    // answered; if a build is owed, this is where it happens.
    //
    // `uris` and `docs` are the server's open documents — the list
    // because a `Map` in this tree cannot be walked, and both because
    // the overlay must carry EVERY unsaved buffer and not only the one
    // that was edited. A build that read the disk for the others would
    // report errors the user has already fixed in a file they can see.
    settled* = (self :: @Self, env: Env, workspace: str, uris: Vec<str>,
                docs: Map<str, str>, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
        self.owed.is_empty().match({
            true  => written(),
            false => self.build_owed(env, workspace, uris, docs, out),
        })
    }

    // NO WORKSPACE, NO DIAGNOSTICS, and this is a refusal rather than a
    // gap. Without a root there is nothing to build but the open file
    // standing alone, and a lone-module check reports every imported
    // name as undefined — which for any real file is a screen of red
    // about a program that is fine. Hover can degrade to the lone
    // module because its failure mode is silence; this one's failure
    // mode is noise, so it does not degrade at all.
    //
    // A ROOT OF NOTHING IS NOT A ROOT either. `root_for` climbs out of
    // the document's own directory and stops at the workspace, so a
    // document that is not under the workspace at all climbs to "" —
    // which is a client whose two halves disagree, not a program.
    build_owed = (self :: @Self, env: Env, workspace: str, uris: Vec<str>,
                  docs: Map<str, str>, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
        uri = self.owed;
        self.owed = "";
        path = path_of(uri);
        root = root_for(env, self.a, workspace, path);
        (workspace.is_empty() || root.is_empty()).match({
            true  => written(),
            false => self.told(env, root, path, uri, uris, docs, out),
        })
    }

    told = (self :: @Self, env: Env, root: str, path: str, uri: str,
            uris: Vec<str>, docs: Map<str, str>, out :: String)
           Res<(), AllocError> {
        spots ::= self.a.Vec<Spot>();
        found_in(env, self.a, root, path, overlay_of(self.a, uris, docs).try(),
                 spots).try();
        self.say_all(env, uri, uris, docs, spots, out)
    }

    // THE THREE SETS, in order: the edited document, then every other
    // file this build found something in, then every file that was
    // showing errors and is not in either.
    say_all = (self :: @Self, env: Env, uri: str, uris: Vec<str>,
               docs: Map<str, str>, spots: Vec<Spot>, out :: String)
              Res<(), AllocError> {
        now ::= self.a.Vec<str>();
        uris_of(spots, now).try();
        say_one(env, self.a, uri, uris, docs, spots, out).try();
        now.loop((h, u) {
            u.eq(uri).match({
                true  => written(),
                false => say_one(env, self.a, u, uris, docs, spots, out),
            }).try();
        });
        self.take_back(env, uri, now, uris, docs, spots, out).try();
        self.showing = now;
        Ok(());
    }

    // AN EMPTY LIST FOR EVERY FILE THAT HAS STOPPED BEING WRONG. The
    // edited document is excluded because it was published first,
    // whether or not it had anything — publishing it twice would be one
    // frame saying two things.
    take_back = (self :: @Self, env: Env, uri: str, now: Vec<str>,
                 uris: Vec<str>, docs: Map<str, str>, spots: Vec<Spot>,
                 out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
        self.showing.loop((h, u) {
            gone(u, uri, now).match({
                false => written(),
                true  => say_one(env, self.a, u, uris, docs, spots, out),
            }).try();
        });
        Ok(());
    }
}

Diagnostics* = (a: Alloc) Diagnostics {
    Diagnostics(a: a, showing: a.Vec<str>())
}

// Was this URI showing errors and is it not among the ones that have
// them now?
gone = (u: str, uri: str, now: Vec<str>) bool {
    u.eq(uri).match({
        true  => false,
        false => now.find((n) { n.eq(u) }).match({ Ok(_) => false, None => true }),
    })
}

// --- the build ---------------------------------------------------------

// A WHOLE BUILD, AND EVERYTHING THREE PHASES FOUND.
//
// The shape is `lsp_hover.zen`'s `hover_in` deliberately: the same
// `Build.whole`, the same overlay, the same computed root. What differs
// is only which of the build's outputs is read — hover wants the memos
// and this wants the diagnostics, and BOTH halves are wanted here
// because a server that showed type errors and silently swallowed
// syntax errors would teach a user to distrust it.
//
// `speaking` IS ALREADY FALSE UNDER `whole`, which is what stops the
// driver's `println` from landing in the middle of a frame stream. That
// is the driver's guarantee and not this file's; what this file relies
// on is the other half of the same change — that the diagnostics are
// still COLLECTED while they are not said.
found_in* = (env: Env, a: Alloc, root: str, path: str, docs: Map<str, str>,
             spots :: Vec<Spot>) Res<(), AllocError> {
    b ::= a.Build(env, root);
    c = b.whole(relative_to(root, path), docs).try();
    from_build(b, a, root, spots).try();
    from_check(c, a, root, spots)
}

// LEX AND PARSE, off the `Build`. `Diag.span.file` is the module's path
// relative to the compilation root, which is exactly what a URI is
// built from.
from_build = (b :: Build, a: Alloc, root: str, spots :: Vec<Spot>)
             Res<(), AllocError> {
    Range(0, b.diag_count()).loop((h, i) {
        b.diag_at(i).match({
            None  => written(),
            Ok(d) => add_parse(a, root, d.message, d.span, d.note, spots),
        }).try();
    });
    Ok(());
}

add_parse = (a: Alloc, root: str, said: str, span: Span, note: Res<Note>,
             spots :: Vec<Spot>) Res<(), AllocError> {
    spots.add(Spot(uri: uri_at(a, root, span.file).try(), message: said,
                   span: span, note: note))
}

// SEMA, off the `Checker` `whole` handed back — which is the whole
// reason `Build.checked` returns a value instead of dropping one.
//
// THE SENTENCE IS RENDERED HERE, while the store that decided it is
// still in hand, and it is `message` plus `write_detail` rather than
// `render`: `render` opens with `file:line:col:`, and a position
// written into a message that already travels beside a `range` is the
// same fact said twice, once in a form no client can click.
from_check = (c :: Checker, a: Alloc, root: str, spots :: Vec<Spot>)
             Res<(), AllocError> {
    Range(0, c.diag_count()).loop((h, i) {
        c.diag_at(i).match({
            None  => written(),
            Ok(d) => add_sema(c, a, root, d.file, d.span, d.fault, spots),
        }).try();
    });
    Ok(());
}

add_sema = (c :: Checker, a: Alloc, root: str, file: str, span: Span,
            fault: SemaFault, spots :: Vec<Spot>) Res<(), AllocError> {
    said ::= a.String().try();
    said.add_bytes(message(fault)).try();
    write_detail(fault, c.types, said).try();
    spots.add(Spot(uri: uri_at(a, root, file).try(), message: said.view(),
                   span: span, note: None))
}

// Every open buffer, keyed the way `Build.read` looks one up: on the
// path, which is what `Unit.path` holds and what `path_of` produces.
overlay_of = (a: Alloc, uris: Vec<str>, docs: Map<str, str>)
             Res<Map<str, str>, AllocError> {
    over ::= a.Map<str, str>();
    uris.loop((h, u) {
        docs.get(u).match({
            None     => written(),
            Ok(text) => over.set(path_of(u), text),
        }).try();
    });
    Ok(over);
}

// The distinct URIs these spots name, in the order they were found.
uris_of = (spots: Vec<Spot>, into :: Vec<str>) Res<(), AllocError> {
    spots.loop((h, s) {
        into.find((u) { u.eq(s.uri) }).match({
            Ok(_) => written(),
            None  => into.add(s.uri),
        }).try();
    });
    Ok(());
}

// --- the notification --------------------------------------------------

// ONE FILE'S WHOLE LIST, framed. Called for a file with nothing to say
// as well as for one with plenty — an empty `diagnostics` array is not
// a degenerate case here, it is the only way a client is told an error
// is gone.
//
// THE TEXT IS THE FILE'S OWN. A span is a byte column into the file it
// came from, so converting it against the open document's bytes would
// underline the right file at a position from another one. An open
// buffer is preferred over the disk for the same reason the build reads
// it: the spans were computed from those bytes.
say_one = (env: Env, a: Alloc, uri: str, uris: Vec<str>, docs: Map<str, str>,
           spots: Vec<Spot>, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    publish(a, uri, text_of(env, a, uri, docs), spots, out)
}

// The bytes the spans in `uri` were measured against: the open buffer if
// there is one, the disk otherwise, and "" when neither answers.
//
// "" IS NOT A SILENT FAILURE. `to_wire` clamps, so every position in an
// unreadable file converts to 0:0 — the diagnostic still reaches the
// client, still names the file and still carries its sentence, and only
// the underline is at the top. A dropped diagnostic would be the worse
// half of that trade, since the file that could not be read is one the
// build has already complained about.
text_of = (env: Env, a: Alloc, uri: str, docs: Map<str, str>) str {
    docs.get(uri).match({
        Ok(text) => text,
        None     => on_disk(env, a, path_of(uri)),
    })
}

on_disk = (env: Env, a: Alloc, path: str) str {
    env.fs.read(a, path).match({
        Ok(text) => text.view(),
        Err(_)   => "",
    })
}

// `textDocument/publishDiagnostics`, as the protocol spells it.
//
// A NOTIFICATION AND NOT A REPLY: no `id`, and nothing is expected
// back. It is the one message this server sends unprompted.
publish* = (a: Alloc, uri: str, text: str, spots: Vec<Spot>, out :: String)
           Res<(), AllocError> {
    body ::= a.String().try();
    body.add_bytes("{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"textDocument/publishDiagnostics\",\"params\":{\"uri\":").try();
    write_text(uri, body).try();
    body.add_bytes(",\"diagnostics\":[").try();
    n ::= 0;
    spots.loop((h, s) { n = one_of(s, uri, text, n, body).try(); });
    body.add_bytes("]}}").try();
    write_frame(body.view(), out)
}

// This spot, if it belongs to this file, and the count so it can tell
// whether a comma comes first.
one_of = (s: Spot, uri: str, text: str, n: usize, out :: String)
         Res<usize, AllocError> {
    s.uri.eq(uri).match({
        false => Ok(n),
        true  => written_at(s, text, n, out),
    })
}

written_at = (s: Spot, text: str, n: usize, out :: String)
             Res<usize, AllocError> {
    comma(n, out).try();
    write_spot(s, text, out).try();
    Ok(n + 1);
}

comma = (n: usize, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    (n > 0).match({ true  => out.add_bytes(","), false => written() })
}

write_spot = (s: Spot, text: str, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    out.add_bytes("{\"range\":").try();
    write_range(text, s.span, out).try();
    out.add_bytes(",\"severity\":").try();
    write_decimal(ERROR, out).try();
    out.add_bytes(",\"source\":\"zen\",\"message\":").try();
    write_text(s.message, out).try();
    write_note(s, text, out).try();
    out.add_bytes("}")
}

// THE NOTE, AS THE SECOND PLACE TO LOOK — see the header for why it is
// not glued onto the sentence.
//
// THE NOTE'S URI IS THE DIAGNOSTIC'S, and that is a fact about the
// parser rather than a shortcut: `parse_diag.zen`'s one builder of a
// note is `expect_close`, which anchors on the opening delimiter of the
// same file it is reading. `n.span.file` and `s.uri` name the same
// module, and using the URI already in hand avoids a second path-to-URI
// conversion that could differ from the first.
write_note = (s: Spot, text: str, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    s.note.match({
        None  => written(),
        Ok(n) => write_related(s.uri, n, text, out),
    })
}

write_related = (uri: str, n: Note, text: str, out :: String)
                Res<(), AllocError> {
    out.add_bytes(",\"relatedInformation\":[{\"location\":{\"uri\":").try();
    write_text(uri, out).try();
    out.add_bytes(",\"range\":").try();
    write_range(text, n.span, out).try();
    out.add_bytes("},\"message\":").try();
    write_text(n.message, out).try();
    out.add_bytes("}]")
}

// `DiagnosticSeverity.Error`. See the header: this compiler has one
// tally and no phase produces anything a build survives, so there is
// one severity and this is the constant that changes on the day there
// is a second.
ERROR*: usize = 1
